All the required data is present. Let me now verify the newsletter form works properly. Looking at the HTML, the feedback div is inside the form alongside the input and button. Since I've set flex-wrap: wrap on the form, and the feedback has width: 100% and order: 3, it should wrap below the input and button.

But wait — the email input doesn't have an explicit `order` set, the button doesn't have an explicit `order`, and the feedback has `order: 3`. With flex order defaults being 0, the input and button will render first (order 0), and then the feedback will render after them but below. With width: 100%, it should span the full width. This looks correct.

However, I notice the inline style on the feedback div has `order: 3`. Actually, in flex layout, all items default to order 0, so the input, button (both order 0) will be first, then the feedback (order 3) will be last. But since it wraps, the feedback will be on the second row with width 100%. This should work correctly.

Let me also verify the `data-label` attributes are in the right format for the CSS `::before` pseudo-element to pick them up. In the CSS I have:
```css
.schedule-table tbody td::before {
    content: attr(data-label);
    ...
}
```

And in the HTML I've added `data-label="Day"` and `data-label="Focus"`. This should work.

Let me also check that the carousel "slide-inner" class styling looks good with the new emoji content. The CSS has:
```css
.carousel__slide-inner {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  font-family: var(--font-heading);
  font-size: 3rem;
  font-weight: 900;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  color: rgba(255,255,255,0.15);
}
```

The slide-inner has `flex-direction` defaulting to row. The emoji span and text will be side by side instead of stacked. Let me fix this by adding `flex-direction: column` to the carousel__slide-inner.

Actually, looking at the HTML structure:
```html
<div class="carousel__slide-inner">
  <span style="...">🏃</span>
  STUDIO ACTION
</div>
```

With default flex-direction: row, the emoji and text will be side by side. That's not ideal. Let me change the CSS.

Hmm, but the original slides just had text: "Studio Action" without emojis. The flex direction being row was fine for just text. Now I've added a span+text, so I should either wrap both in a div with column direction, or change the CSS.

Actually, let me just wrap both the emoji and the text in a div with column flex. That's simpler.

Wait, actually the simplest fix is to nest the content: wrap the emoji and text in a wrapping div. But that changes the structure more. Let me just update the CSS for carousel__slide-inner to use column direction. That's the cleanest approach.

